### What is the default pool for supplyAsync?

When you call `supplyAsync()` or `runAsync()` without specifying an executor, the task runs on `ForkJoinPool.commonPool()`. This pool has parallelism equal to `Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors() - 1`.

```java
System.out.println("Common pool parallelism: " +
    ForkJoinPool.commonPool().getParallelism());
// On a 4-core machine: 3
// On an 8-core machine: 7
```

### Why is the common pool dangerous for IO-bound work?

The common pool is shared across the entire JVM. Blocking IO on its few threads starves all other users of the pool.

```java
// Component A: 3 slow JDBC queries on commonPool
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
        return jdbcTemplate.queryForObject("SELECT ...", String.class);
        // Blocks 2 seconds! All 3 commonPool threads occupied!
    });
}

// Component B (UNRELATED): parallel stream for reports
report = accounts.parallelStream()  // ALSO uses commonPool!
    .map(this::generateStatement)    // STALLED! All threads blocked by A!
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
// Report generation waits 2 seconds because JDBC monopolized the pool!
```

### How do you create dedicated executors?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class PoolFactory {
    private static final AtomicInteger ioCounter = new AtomicInteger();
    private static final AtomicInteger cpuCounter = new AtomicInteger();

    // IO pool: many threads for blocking calls
    public static ExecutorService createIoPool() {
        return new ThreadPoolExecutor(
            20, 50, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
            new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(1000),
            r -> {
                Thread t = new Thread(r, "io-worker-" + ioCounter.incrementAndGet());
                t.setDaemon(true);
                return t;
            },
            new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy()
        );
    }

    // CPU pool: cores threads for compute
    public static ExecutorService createCpuPool() {
        return Executors.newFixedThreadPool(
            Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors(),
            r -> new Thread(r, "cpu-worker-" + cpuCounter.incrementAndGet())
        );
    }
}
```

### How do you use dedicated pools with CompletableFuture?

```java
ExecutorService ioPool = PoolFactory.createIoPool();
ExecutorService cpuPool = PoolFactory.createCpuPool();

// IO work on dedicated pool
CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> httpCall(), ioPool);
CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> jdbcQuery(), ioPool);

// CPU work on dedicated pool
CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> encrypt(data), cpuPool);

// commonPool stays free for parallelStream and framework use
```

### What are the benefits of named threads?

Named threads make thread dumps instantly readable. Instead of `ForkJoinPool.commonPool-worker-1`, you see `io-worker-5` or `payment-worker-3`, immediately telling you which pool and which concern is involved.

